Hi Roy,

Thanks for the example code, as well as the textbook! I've explored this issue a bit, see below:

On Thu, 2020-10-29 at 17:17 -0700, Roy Levy wrote:
>
> In specifying this model via blavaan, I was able to fix most parameters to the values above, but ran into errors when trying to fix them all. To get around this, I specified a N(80, .001) prior (in the sd metric) for the factor mean, and a N(0, .001) prior (in the sd metric) for the intercept, which effectively constrains them to be 80, and 0, respectively.

I think this happened because, in the original model specification, there were no free parameters, and blavaan was expecting some free parameters. I should be able to fix that soon.

>
>
> Fitting the model with target=“stan” yields results for the factor scores that are meaningfully different. In particular, the posterior means for the first and third factor score are more extreme than the analytical solution. The second factor score has a posterior mean that is in line with the analytical solution.

I wonder whether you are able to install the version of blavaan on github (which will go on CRAN soon, hopefully)? See the command at the bottom of this page to install:

https://github.com/ecmerkle/blavaan

I have been working on the code for lv sampling recently, and the version on github seems to provide the results that you would expect (even if you only do 200 or so samples). There is one point related to that, which is that the eta parameters will no longer appear in "mcobj". For now, the means and posterior samples are in

blavInspect(fit, 'lvmeans')
blavInspect(fit, 'lvs')

and some summary output similar to what you were looking at before is accessible at

fit@external$stansumm

>
>
> I am unsure as to why this difference occurs. I would be happy to learn that this is tied to the use of the marginal as opposed to the conditional likelihood, and that this behavior is expected. But I confess I don’t understand how that would be, given that there are no other parameters that are estimated in the model.

You are right, the conditional and marginal should not systematically change the results. They are two different representations of the same model.

I will plan to add this example as a unit test, it's not every day that you see analytic results related to these models!

Best,
Ed

